  "summary": "Painterly paste-resist on silk: garden-like kimono patterns outlined in fine rice-paste lines.",
  "description": "Yūzen freed kimono pattern from the repeat: a fine rice-paste line is drawn freehand onto silk, enclosing painterly fields that are brush-dyed in gradients — plum branches bending through shading, gold outline accents, steamed to fix. Attributed to fan-painter Miyazaki Yūzen in the Genroku era, the technique split into Kyo-yuzen (courtly richness) and Kaga-yuzen (browner, melancholic, five-color discipline). Entire garden scenes flow across one furisode; today's living national treasures still draw the paste line by hand.",
